Okay so I want to build an OSINT pipeline to search for jobs, to circumvent modern job boards. Here is the end goal we want to achieve, a markdown checklist of entries, each entry contains a company name and address, if possible the general number of employees the company comprises of (to gauge size), a url to the business's informational/about-the-company portion of their public webpage, and a url to the careers section of their public webpage. I want to gather the info from public sources like public records, google map data, etc to essentially "scour" a city and surrounding area for physically present businesses with web pages. Then the markdown checklist can be used to systematically research and apply for jobs based on this verified list of all businesses. Particularly this works for IT people since most businesses regardless of field will have IT needs that they either take care of in-house or outsource. I want to employ techniques similar to bug bounty recon, to gather the info and parse down urls. I think this list would be a great alternative for someone looking locally and not having success with job boards. And would help people stay organized and have a visual of what is in the area and where they have tried applying, and where is left to look. Which I think is just a much different vibe for the job seeker than job boards. It is more footwork but I think it would feel more productive and give a little control back in a difficult situation.
